# Pattern matching and exhaustiveness

JavaScript has no native pattern matching (TC39 Stage 1). The value is exhaustiveness: the compiler must fail when a variant is added and a branch is missing. Model closed domain state as a discriminated union, not optional flags. Validate unknown input at the boundary; match trusted values in the core.

Use `if` for a boolean or two-line condition. Use an exhaustive `switch` plus `never` in plain TypeScript. Use `ts-pattern` (`match`, `.returnType<T>()`, `.with`, `.exhaustive()`, `P.select()`) for nested domain unions (fill status, order lifecycle). Do not match True Myth `Result` or `Maybe`; chain `map` / `andThen`. Do not decorate simple control flow.

```typescript
import { match, P } from 'ts-pattern';

type FillStatus =
    | { readonly type: 'open' }
    | { readonly type: 'filled'; readonly quantity: number }
    | { readonly type: 'canceled'; readonly reason: string };

function assertNever(value: never): never {
    throw new Error(`Unhandled: ${JSON.stringify(value)}`);
}

// ❌ Optional soup; switch with no never; ts-pattern on Result
type FillFlags = { isFilled?: boolean; quantity?: number };

// ✅ Union; match by shape, or switch with default: assertNever(status)
function fillLabel(status: FillStatus): string {
    return match(status)
        .returnType<string>()
        .with({ type: 'open' }, function open() {
            return 'Open';
        })
        .with({ type: 'filled', quantity: P.select() }, function filled(quantity) {
            return `Filled ${quantity}`;
        })
        .with({ type: 'canceled' }, function canceled(fill) {
            return `Canceled: ${fill.reason}`;
        })
        .exhaustive();
}
```
